WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.450
In our previous videos, we studied the structure
of a human eye and the

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.799
propagation of light through it.

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:15.920
There we saw that most of the light which
enters the eye is REFRACTED by the outer front

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.220
layer of the eye called the
cornea.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.220
What does this biconvex lens do then?

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.630
It refracts light, yes, but its main objective
is to make sure that the image is

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.310
formed on the retina only.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:34.460
By changing its shape, the lens controls the
direction of propagation of light.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.260
This ability of the lens to change its focal
length is defined as the ACCOMMODATION POWER

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.000
of the eye.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.280
This is
something we’d seen in the previous videos!

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.110
In this video, we will discuss few of the
most common eye defects.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.780
Let’s start with Myopia first.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.330
It is one of the most common eye defects and
it is

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.989
also called NEARSIGHTEDNESS.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:00.399
It is a condition of the eye in which the
nearby

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:05.129
objects are visible clearly but the distant
objects are blurry.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.240
How does the light
propagate inside a myopic eye?

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.420
Well the light from the nearer object travels
in the usual way.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.200
It gets focussed
on the retina only.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.179
No problems there!

00:01:18.179 --> 00:01:20.670
What about the distant objects?

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.789
First of all the light rays from a faraway
object coming towards the eye are

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:27.560
almost parallel to each other.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.619
And when these rays enter the eye, they get
converged IN FRONT of the retina instead of

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:34.700
ON the retina.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.539
This is the reason
for blurriness.

00:01:37.539 --> 00:01:40.819
But what causes the light to converge in front
of the retina

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:43.349
rather than ON the retina?

00:01:43.349 --> 00:01:48.159
The larger size of the eye ball is one of
the reasons which results in this eye

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.159
defect.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:52.569
Due to the elongated eye ball, the distance
between the lens and the

00:01:52.569 --> 00:01:55.259
retina is more than the usual.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:57.549
Because of this larger distance between the
two,

00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:59.630
the image is formed in front!

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.989
Well one thing we must note here is that the
lens

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.489
functions in its usual way.

00:02:05.489 --> 00:02:08.630
It does its work neatly as it is supposed
to!

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.610
The only
thing is that the image is formed at a different

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.290
position.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.159
So how do we overcome this eye defect?

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:18.860
It can be corrected by using a
concave lens.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.520
We place a concave lens in front of myopic
eye.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.730
Why concave
lens?

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.530
We know that the concave lens diverges the
light!

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.530
And these divergent

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:35.459
rays fall on the lens at such an angle such
that they refract and come together

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:37.260
ON the retina.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:38.750
Not in front of the retina!

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.810
Ok, so that was all about myopia!

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.760
Let’s quickly review what we learnt.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.549
If a person is suffering from myopia, then
far away objects are not visible clearly.

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:55.260
That’s because the light rays after
entering the eye do not converge exactly on

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.310
the retina!

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:00.099
They converge at a
point in front of the retina!

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:03.340
So a concave lens helps fix this issue.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.239
The rays
diverge, and the refracted rays from the lens

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:08.890
fall ON the retina!

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:12.810
The other most common eye defect and that
is Hyperopia.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.150
What is hyperopia?

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.060
And have you heard of Presbyopia?

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.909
Watch the next lesson to know more!

